WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.736
Language, images, and sound are all modeled as discrete tokens.

00:00:04.736 --> 00:00:09.750
Robot actions are the exception: most policies predict continuous numbers.

00:00:09.750 --> 00:00:13.548
The worry is that cutting actions into bins throws away precision.

00:00:14.618 --> 00:00:18.548
So, are robot actions special, or can discrete actions compete?

00:00:18.548 --> 00:00:23.226
To discretize, we snap each step to a cell of a grid, one bin per axis.

00:00:25.196 --> 00:00:32.901
To answer the question, we compare four action heads along two axes: continuous or discrete, and one pass or iterative.

00:00:32.901 --> 00:00:39.692
Continuous regression predicts the chunk in one pass, so when demonstrations disagree, it predicts their average.

00:00:39.692 --> 00:00:46.309
Continuous flow starts from Gaussian noise and follows a learned velocity toward the data, over several steps.

00:00:46.309 --> 00:00:50.567
The discrete heads cut each number's range into 512 bins.

00:00:50.567 --> 00:00:59.885
Discrete regression gives each position its own distribution over bins, trained with cross-entropy, and picks the most likely bin everywhere in one pass.

00:00:59.885 --> 00:01:08.726
Discrete flow starts from random tokens, and at each step, positions jump to new proposals, so a token can be revised as the chunk takes shape.

00:01:08.726 --> 00:01:13.054
Everything else is matched: the backbone, the data, and the action-head size.

00:01:14.424 --> 00:01:20.240
We test them at three scales. First, single-task policies trained from scratch on RoboMimic.

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:27.674
Then a PaliGemma VLA, post-trained on RoboCasa, DROID, and MimicGen, and tested on RoboCasa and LIBERO.

00:01:27.674 --> 00:01:29.813
And three tasks on a real robot.

00:01:29.813 --> 00:01:33.287
Discrete flow matches continuous flow across the board.

00:01:33.287 --> 00:01:38.097
It holds up on the precision-heavy RoboMimic tasks, Tool Hang and Transport.

00:01:38.097 --> 00:01:41.635
On the real robot, it ties or wins on all three tasks.

00:01:41.635 --> 00:01:49.185
Even the simplest discrete head, one-pass classification over bins, beats continuous regression on nearly every benchmark.

00:01:50.555 --> 00:01:52.831
So are discrete heads just more precise?

00:01:52.831 --> 00:01:53.995
Not quite.

00:01:53.995 --> 00:01:59.583
On held-out demonstrations, end-effector error is within three millimeters for all four heads.

00:01:59.583 --> 00:02:06.989
And it does not track success: continuous regression has the lowest error, but trails both flow heads by over ten points.

00:02:06.989 --> 00:02:10.049
What differs is how much the predicted actions vary.

00:02:10.049 --> 00:02:14.264
Jerk measures how fast the motion changes, using four steps in a row.

00:02:14.264 --> 00:02:17.911
The jerk ratio divides the predicted jerk by the expert's.

00:02:17.911 --> 00:02:20.220
Below one means the chunk is smoothed out.

00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:23.931
Continuous regression sits far below one: it over-smooths.

00:02:23.931 --> 00:02:26.512
Discrete regression comes closest to one.

00:02:26.512 --> 00:02:32.330
Jerk looks inside one chunk. Responsiveness looks across chunks, with the same kind of ratio.

00:02:32.330 --> 00:02:38.126
Take two nearby states, and divide how much a policy's chunk changes by how much the expert's does.

00:02:38.126 --> 00:02:43.255
Discrete regression is almost as responsive as the expert, with discrete flow next.

00:02:43.255 --> 00:02:45.820
Both continuous heads respond much less.

00:02:47.190 --> 00:02:52.924
If discrete regression responds so well, why does it still fall behind both flow heads? There is a catch.

00:02:52.924 --> 00:02:56.777
Take Push-T: the pusher starts right on the T's mirror line.

00:02:56.777 --> 00:03:00.317
So going around either side works: mode A or mode B.

00:03:00.317 --> 00:03:04.464
Discrete regression picks each step on its own, without looking at the others.

00:03:04.464 --> 00:03:08.096
Early in the chunk, most of the probability is on mode B.

00:03:08.096 --> 00:03:10.737
Around step seven, mode A takes over.

00:03:10.737 --> 00:03:14.873
So the chunk follows mode B first, then switches to mode A partway.

00:03:14.873 --> 00:03:16.942
The switch cuts straight across the T.

00:03:16.942 --> 00:03:19.105
Each choice is plausible on its own.

00:03:19.105 --> 00:03:22.423
Together, they form a chunk no demonstrator would make.

00:03:22.423 --> 00:03:24.513
We call this factorization error.

00:03:25.883 --> 00:03:27.803
We find two ways to fix it.

00:03:27.803 --> 00:03:30.226
The first is classifier-free guidance.

00:03:30.226 --> 00:03:36.295
Guidance runs the model twice, with and without the observation, and boosts what the observation adds.

00:03:36.295 --> 00:03:39.512
Different tokens in a chunk can favor different modes.

00:03:39.512 --> 00:03:42.529
Without guidance, a few tokens pick the other mode.

00:03:42.529 --> 00:03:46.946
Each token picks on its own, so the chunk mixes modes, and the task fails.

00:03:46.946 --> 00:03:51.236
Strong guidance commits every token to one mode, so the whole chunk agrees.

00:03:51.236 --> 00:03:54.580
Guidance nearly doubles the success of discrete regression.

00:03:54.580 --> 00:03:58.488
Yet it does not make actions more precise: the error stays the same.

00:03:58.488 --> 00:04:03.332
So guidance works by fixing factorization error, not by improving precision.

00:04:03.332 --> 00:04:06.103
The second fix is iterative computation.

00:04:06.103 --> 00:04:12.458
Discrete flow refines its tokens over several rounds, and each round sees the tokens chosen so far.

00:04:12.458 --> 00:04:17.884
This fixes the factorization error, and discrete flow reaches the highest success of any head.

00:04:17.884 --> 00:04:23.776
And it keeps its action diversity: the tokens agree, but the draws do not collapse to one answer.

00:04:25.146 --> 00:04:32.395
So far, every discrete head used plain uniform bins. A common worry is that such flat bins have no sense of space.

00:04:32.395 --> 00:04:40.671
To the model, bin 200 and bin 201 are just unrelated labels, so it might memorize answers and generalize poorly.

00:04:40.671 --> 00:04:47.535
We tried telling it which bins are close, with a distance-aware corruption path, label smoothing, and learned tokenizers.

00:04:47.535 --> 00:04:50.646
None beats plain uniform bins with discrete flow.

00:04:50.646 --> 00:04:58.210
The model already learns the geometry: neighboring bins get similar output embeddings, and the similarity fades with distance.

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:04.557
Let's put it all together. When do discrete actions make good continuous controllers?

00:05:04.557 --> 00:05:06.167
When their tokens agree.

00:05:06.167 --> 00:05:08.297
Simple bins are precise enough.

00:05:08.297 --> 00:05:13.822
The hard part is getting tokens to agree, and guidance and iterative refinement solve much of it.

00:05:13.822 --> 00:05:18.438
This puts robot actions in the same token framework as language and vision.

00:05:18.438 --> 00:05:24.697
It also opens a new question: tokenizers built for consistency, not only for reconstruction.
